BUGS:

(1.32 fixed) After a review of 1.31 by another session: check_file did
    not make its path canonical, so a trailing slash or .. recorded junk
    paths (new exposure from the 1.31 check_file fix); update and some
    warnings printed file names unescaped; some changes errors (bad
    options, -k) did not exit 2; on OpenBSD with immutable-specs: no,
    flags were recorded as undefined (chflags and ls not unveiled); under
    privsep check -v always said there were no extraneous spec-dir files;
    verify_hosts did not check that a spec holds the tree its name is for.

(1.31 fixed) Partial update (or initialize) of some trees left their
    paths in the changed file's per-set lists, so changes -v (and
    check's summary) still listed changes to trees already updated.

(1.31 fixed) With -m, skipping a macOS app content path in the summary
    put the following paths' changed attribute names out of step.

(1.31 fixed) -m left changes inside apps out of the summary instead of
    summarizing them (an app whose bundle dir hadn't changed vanished),
    and check -v still listed them one by one. Now counted per app.

(1.31 fixed) check recorded no check time for the spec dir, so changes
    died (exit 2) whenever check had found the spec dir changed.

(1.31 fixed) A check with -s (or check_file, or check_specs) replaced
    all pending changes in the changed file, losing other trees' changes
    not yet reviewed or updated. Now only what was checked is replaced.

(1.31 fixed) check_file checked only the spec dir, never the file named
    (its tree was dropped when trees were chosen by set), and for a
    directory below a tree's root, its entries' paths were wrong.

(1.31 fixed) if a tree is removed from config while present in the
    changed file: check already dropped it (reset_changed_file), and
    update now always removes it (it stopped scanning the changed file
    at the first tree to update, so it was removed only sometimes).

(1.28a fixed) Fix bug introduced in 1.28 for Linux that causes
    extraneous error messages on flag checking.
    
(1.27 fixed) Fix bug introduced in 1.26 that causes no changedfile
    output if a single file has a read error.

(1.26 fixed) Claude Opus 5.5 review:
    call Signify::sign and Signify::verify with Signify.pm's named
    arguments and require Signify.pm 1.4 (1.3 can report a successful
    signing with a signify -n key as failed). Privileged parent:
    is_allowed_path rejects ".." components, so the unprivileged process
    can no longer have root open any file via "$tree/../.."; removed the
   /tmp/sigtree.* exception from is_sigtree_managed_file (unused under
    privsep), through which the unprivileged process could have root
    run chflags on a symlink it placed in /tmp; chflags now runs with -h
    so a symlink is never followed; signing and verification use the
    parent's own method and keys, not the request's; the passphrase it
    receives is overwritten after use. A failed signature (signify or
    PGP) now stops initialize and update, and update signs a spec before
    removing its tree from the changed file, so a wrong passphrase no
    longer leaves specs with stale signatures and the changes forgotten.
    initialize and update also sign a throwaway file right after the
    passphrase prompt (in the privileged parent under privsep), so a
    wrong passphrase stops them before any spec is changed.
    initialize and check now treat a worker that died as failed (its
    tree's changes are kept; check does not write a changed file with a
    tree missing) and exit non-zero. chflags/chattr failures are
    reported and fatal, including under privsep, where the request used
    to report success; "Unable to reset" no longer exits 0. Securelevel
    -1 is accepted and treated as 0; sysctl failures are reported.
    update's "Failed to load spec ... skipping" now skips only that tree
    (it ended the update with the spec dir unlocked). An absolute -d
    outside the root dir now works under privsep (the privileged parent
    refused the host spec, which is in the spec dir's parent) and its
    spec dir is in the sigtree set (it had no set). An absolute
    exception or exception-tree path is now a config error (it never
    matched, and with an ignored set gave no warning), and an
    exception-tree for the whole tree says to change the tree's sets
    instead. unveil: the spec
    dir's parent is unveiled when an absolute -d puts it outside the
    root dir; dropped x where nothing is exec'd and /bin/sh for the
    immutable commands (ls is run directly). Removed the Linux-only FD
    ACK (reads are exact-length sysread) and bounded the descriptor wait
    at 30 seconds instead; SIGPIPE is ignored around socket writes; a
    lost or corrupt privsep response is fatal instead of read as
    success. Worker temp changed files are marked by the caller rather
    than by a /tmp/ path prefix, and their temp dir is removed even
    after a worker died with its file half-written. Spec and changed
    file writes check close. yes_or_no treats end of input as no; no passphrase at end of
    input is an error. The passphrase is overwritten at the end of
    initialize and update (the 1 May wipe applied only to an empty
    string). require 5.010, which the code already needed.

(1.25 fixed) Claude Opus 4.8 review:
	- Fixed privilege-separation readdir failure: request_readdir read
	the response under key 'entries_b64', but handle_readdir_request
	returns it as 'entries', causing an undef array deref (fatal under
	strict) on any directory the unprivileged user cannot read
	directly (e.g. the 0700 spec dir). Now reads 'entries', with a
	defensive || [].
	- Fixed missing "/" in the host-spec existence
	test in verify_required_dirs
	(was $spec_dir_dir . "$spec_spec"), which had made the host-spec
	readability/writability checks unreachable dead code.
	- Fixed wrong-case hash key in FileAttr::compare: an nlink
	difference stored $fileattr->{nlink} (undef) instead of {NLINK},
	suppressing display of nlink changes even though the change was
	still detected.
	- Fixed ChangedFile::reset_changed_file initializing its hash
	fields with = () (which yields undef) instead of = {}. TIME/USER
	are intentionally left un-reset so the history of prior,
	not-yet-updated checks is preserved.
	- Fixed a dead load-failure check in check_sets that tested
	$changed_file (the filename, always true) instead of $changedfile
	(the object).
	- Hardened privileged and nonprivileged file reads against
	symlink/TOCTOU swaps: added O_NOFOLLOW (and O_NONBLOCK) to read
	opens; special-cased ELOOP/EMLINK to report a file whose type
	changed between stat and open (the nonpriv path warns and skips
	the privileged fallback rather than masking the swap); and added
	an fstat dev/ino comparison against the classifying lstat to catch
	intermediate-directory symlink and inode swaps.  Added a "refusing
	to write through symlink" message on the write path.
	- Removed dead IPC helpers request_write_file/request_read_file,
	which sent WRITE_FILE/READ_FILE request types that no handler
	dispatched.
	- Replaced regex-based exact matching of tree paths
	(grep /^$tree$/) with string eq / quotemeta, to avoid mismatching
	paths that contain regex metacharacters; fixed incomplete (non-/g)
	escaping in tree-path matching.
	- Fixed a fork-count off-by-one that compared $#specified_trees
	(last index) to the requested child count, under-parallelizing by
	one and disabling forking entirely with two trees.
	- Tightened config validation of
	max_child_procs/default_child_procs (anchored the integer regex;
	removed a redundant assignment).
	- Corrected a stale comment in recv_request (the limit is 1MB,
          not 10MB).


(1.24c fixed) Do config writeability permissions check, move
overly permissive readability check into Config package.

(1.24b fixed) Use right location for sysctl (/usr/sbin) on macOS
and right format for securelevel output on macOS (: instead of =).
Change process names of child processes.

(1.24a fixes) Added interrupt handler to passphrase input to
restore tty echo. Make macos.sigtree.conf name all lower case.

(1.24 fixes) Added safe_open_for_write helper to open and lock files
for writing (for changed files and specs) to not follow symlinks,
modified handle_open_request to not follow symlinks for privileged
opens for writing, tightened is_sigtree_managed_file checks to reject
directory traversal and be stricter about filenames, wipe $pgp_passphrase
from memory after use in get_pgp_passphrase.

(1.23a fixes) Added warning for config file and spec permissions
when group- and world-readable.

(1.23 fixes) Bug in critical-log (mtimestasis keyword) handling.

(1.21a fixes) Bug in handling link targets.

(1.20b fixes) Initialize on /dev takes forever for Proxmox/Debian Linux
(problem is /dev/core, default config now ignores).

(1.20b fixes) When -s <set name> is used, messages are still displayed for forking
children that are not being initialized.

(DONE 1.31) when reporting change in mode, show the changed mode?
(Changed values of all attributes are now stored by check and shown in
its summary and by changes.)

Doesn't report SHA digest changes or directory file changes for link targets.

Errors can sometimes occur if -s <bogussetname> is used.

If /var/db/sigtree/spec/host or /var/db/sigtree/secondary/spec/host is
in the changed file, initialize_specs doesn't take it out. (Have modified
update to permit updating a changed spec directory with version 1.14.)

FEATURES TO ADD:

verify_hosts reference files: keep the signed spec as the authority but
also report whether the current snapshot already has the new copy
(fixed in place, awaiting update), and optionally compare other per-host
copies via a configurable reference-copies: /var/db/servers/%h. All
locations configurable.

More informative config error messages: one pass over all config
parsing (sigtree and verify_hosts) so that each error says what was
expected (e.g., missing :set on an exception-tree: line).

verify_hosts: common-if-present: (compare only where two or more peers
have the file) -- not needed so far; reference: naming another host
(host:path) as the authority; subset check of chroot etc/passwd and
etc/group against the host's files; a way to lift the 25-entry list
cap for exploratory runs.

(DONE 1.31) verify_hosts detail query steps forward past unverifiable
(GPG-era) snapshot specs, hides mtime/ctime unless -v, -k to choose
attributes. check stores old and new values in the changed file, shown
in check's summary and changes -v; plain changes shows attribute names;
changes spec | /path shows values and reports changes since undone or
changed again; -k for changes; display code shared with verify_hosts.

Add sign_specs that signs (or re-signs) them all, perhaps as an option
to initialize_specs. Currently the only way to do that is re-initializing
each tree (i.e., initialize_specs doesn't do it).

Set user immutable or +i on changed file if immutable flags are used.

(DONE 1.30) No longer delete changed files when empty (use for verify_hosts),
make changes exit 0 if changes are pending, 1 if changed file is empty,
other nonzero for errors. verify_hosts rollback check walks snapshots between
the previous and current, verify_hosts with a spec will show what paths
changed and with a path will compare, if possible to snapshots to show
an actual diff. Record device numbers for device nodes in specs. Do
chroot checks with verify_hosts when configured.

(DONE 1.29) Add a way to run check against a non-local host using a copy of the
specs, that has a different file system prefix (e.g., a backup or
separately mounted file system); it would pre-pend the prefix on the
files being checked. (Also support for update?) Need to think through
the pros and cons of verifying rsynced specs from the original host vs.
just running a separate initialize/check/update on the backup, which
would require a separate config. Implemented as verify_hosts with its
own separate config.

(DONE 1.28) Improve performance by getting flags in batches;
add primary- and secondary- versions of exception and exception-tree.

(DONE 1.22) Optional privilege separation, with -p or privsep: yes in
config file. Requires a _sigtree user and group.  Sigtree-managed
files remain owned by root.

(DONE) Allow forking child processes to run check_tree (e.g., using
Parallel::ForkManager; it will require adding 'proc' to pledge
and pledge can potentially be ratcheted down for the child
processes (which should inherit the same pledge/unveil of the
parent).
(DONE) Ditto for initialize.
(DONE)ToDo: add -f <N> option to specify number of children to allow (0-5?).

Allow sigtree to be used to check backups of multiple hosts, which
would require a --host <hostname> and --altroot <altroot-prefix>
option to check, check_file, check_specs.  (prefix would have to be
stuck on the front of each tree and unveiled). Also allow a random
sample (random files from random trees in a possibly specified set)
to be checked with check_file. [The original point of different hostname
dirs was to allow specs from multiple hosts to be in one place, but
it's not really practical unless implemented within sigtree itself
somehow, at least when immutability is used. Maybe import specs from
backup/check backup? Note that I don't backup some things that are
monitored -- e.g., binaries.]]

Should do more consistent escaping of potentially dangerous characters in filenames
that will be used in command lines. (DONE)

Could use append-only file flags to create checkpoint files for hashes
of spec files, which are verified at time of update (sappnd for primary,
uappnd for secondary, perhaps--or sappnd for secondary also).

On macOS have a mode or report that doesn't report details below directories
named "<name>.app". (DONE; fixed in 1.31 to count changes per app
rather than omit them)

Allow doing comparison of the contents of a sigtree database against a set
of directories that may be mounted or on an altroot, so that there is a
prefix on the directory name. This would probably need to be limited to
check, no initialize or update, and would probably require passing an
extra parameter around through a number of functions and packages.

Allow importing hashes from mtree and pkg db? Or sha2/3_create files?
  Or via config file reference to same? (Import sounds better.)
Add to config file a reference to file of system SHA hashes, and ability to
   check against that (hash check only). Ability to create such a file?
   (i.e., incorporate sha3_create/compare capability).

Add use of user immutability (uchg) for changed files.

Add ability to re-initialize signatures for all specs (initialize_specs doesn't
and probably shouldn't do that).

Add ability to auto-generate a configuration via interactive questions
about directories off the root directory (descending the tree on
demand).

Add ability to send notifications, with email addresses definable
per-set and per-tree. (Or more general API interface to functionality.)

Add ability to initialize new trees added to the conf file without
initializing anything else or having to put those trees into a special
set.  Perhaps create an implicit set called "new" or "uncreated" that
includes all and only trees without specifications. (Or via interactive
browsing feature.) ('new" set DONE)

Per-user sigtree conf files (~/.sigtree.conf ?), when run as non-root.
Allow setting root dir, spec dir in ~/.sigtree.conf; perhaps also
via environmental variables.

Add keyword(s) for cyclog/multilog dirs, so that it won't keep
commenting on every new addition to/deletion from the log directory
unless it somehow violates the format of a cyclog/multilog dir.
The reason this can't currently be done with exceptions is that
there is no flag to ignore file adds/deletions.  (With regexps,
you could get close, by having it ignore files in the directory
that are of the appropriate format.) [This can now be done with exceptions
using the "ignore" set:  exception-tree: logdir:IGN.]

Allow the ability to use reg exps (or limited regexps) in exception:
fields.  (Will require changing the subroutines underlying the
determination of primary set...)  Useful for, e.g., measuring
different things based on file suffixes.

Create an API layer to expose functionality for other tools.

Add functionality to facilitate remote execution (i.e., keep 
specifications on a secure bastion host, scp them (and the code)
out to each remote host, run the check, and summarize changes
for all hosts; run updates on all hosts and scp the specifications
back to the bastion host).  Use a client/server architecture.
GUI?  Showing hosts, files changed/added/deleted?

Or: add ability to detect changes in real-time and report back
to a central server.

Add the ability to attempt to correlate file changes with entries in
logs (in particular, in the sudo log --/var/log/security, where we can
come up with a username, and perhaps also with /var/log/messages).

Add support for Windows and MacOS.  (Will need to use File::Basename
in a lot of places.)  (Actually, this should already work on Mac OS X
now--or Cygwin.)

Add the ability to generate an mtree file from a spec. (low priority)
(And vice versa?)
